(>> 4.1 P-N Junction Diodes and Zener Voltage Regulators) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(Semiconductor physics enables active electronic amplification and digital computation.) Tj
T*
(* P-N Junction Diode: Formed by joining p-type \(acceptor-doped, majority holes\) and) Tj
T*
(n-type \(donor-doped, majority electrons\) semiconductor regions. Majority carriers) Tj
T*
(diffuse across the metallurgical junction, leaving uncompensated ionized dopants) Tj
T*
(forming a space-charge Depletion Region. This creates a built-in barrier potential) Tj
T*
(V_bi \(0.7V for Silicon, 0.3V for Germanium\).) Tj
T*
(* Biasing: Forward bias \(positive terminal to p-type\) narrows depletion width,) Tj
T*
(enabling exponential diffusion current: I = I_0 * [ e^{V / \(eta * V_T\)} - 1 ].) Tj
T*
(Reverse bias widens depletion width, reducing current to tiny reverse saturation) Tj
T*
(current I_0.) Tj
T*
(* Zener Diode: Heavily doped diode exhibiting sharp reverse breakdown. Breakdown) Tj
T*
(mechanisms: \(1\) Zener breakdown \(dominant < 5V: high electric field rips covalent) Tj
T*
(bonds\), \(2\) Avalanche breakdown \(dominant > 6V: minority carriers accelerate,) Tj
T*
(impact-ionizing lattice atoms\). Used in reverse bias as a precision shunt voltage) Tj
T*
(regulator maintaining constant terminal voltage V_z despite variations in supply) Tj
T*
(voltage or load current.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
