([SOLVED PROBLEM 1: Zener Diode Shunt Voltage Regulator Design]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem: A 10V Zener diode is used in a shunt regulator circuit with unregulated DC) Tj
T*
(input voltage V_in varying between 15V and 20V. Load resistor R_L varies from 500 Omega) Tj
T*
(to 1,000 Omega. The minimum Zener knee current is I_z,min = 5 mA. Calculate: \(1\) Value) Tj
T*
(of series current-limiting resistor R_s, \(2\) Maximum power dissipated in the Zener) Tj
T*
(diode.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 1: Determine Load Currents:) Tj
T*
(Regulated output voltage V_out = V_z = 10 V.) Tj
T*
(Minimum load current: I_{L, min} = V_z / R_{L, max} = 10 V / 1000 Omega = 10 mA.) Tj
T*
(Maximum load current: I_{L, max} = V_z / R_{L, min} = 10 V / 500 Omega = 20 mA.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 2: Calculate Series Resistor R_s:) Tj
T*
(Worst-case condition for maintaining regulation occurs when V_in is at its minimum \(15V\)) Tj
T*
(and load current is at its maximum \(20 mA\), requiring Zener to maintain minimum knee) Tj
T*
(current \(5 mA\):) Tj
T*
(Total supply current I_{s, min} = I_{L, max} + I_{z, min} = 20 mA + 5 mA = 25 mA = 0.025) Tj
T*
(A.) Tj
T*
(R_s = \(V_{in, min} - V_z\) / I_{s, min} = \(15 - 10\) / 0.025 = 5 / 0.025 = 200 Omega.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 3: Maximum Zener Dissipation:) Tj
T*
(Occurs when V_in is maximum \(20V\) and load current is minimum \(10 mA\):) Tj
T*
(I_{s, max} = \(V_{in, max} - V_z\) / R_s = \(20 - 10\) / 200 = 10 / 200 = 50 mA = 0.050 A.) Tj
T*
(Maximum Zener Current: I_{z, max} = I_{s, max} - I_{L, min} = 50 mA - 10 mA = 40 mA.) Tj
T*
(Maximum Zener Power: P_{z, max} = V_z * I_{z, max} = 10 V * 0.040 A = 0.40 Watts = 400) Tj
T*
(mW.) Tj
T*
(Safety check: A standard 0.5W \(500 mW\) Zener diode is safely rated for this design.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
([SOLVED PROBLEM 2: BJT Common-Emitter DC Operating Point \(Q-Point\)]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem: In a fixed-bias BJT circuit, V_CC = 12 V, R_B = 470 kOmega, R_C = 2.2 kOmega,) Tj
T*
(and Beta = 100. Assuming a silicon transistor with V_BE = 0.7 V, calculate: \(1\) Base) Tj
T*
(current I_B, \(2\) Collector current I_C, \(3\) Collector-Emitter voltage V_CE, \(4\) Identify) Tj
T*
(whether the transistor operates in Active or Saturation region.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 1: Calculate Base Current I_B:) Tj
T*
(KVL around base-emitter loop: V_CC - I_B * R_B - V_BE = 0.) Tj
T*
(I_B = \(V_CC - V_BE\) / R_B = \(12 - 0.7\) / \(470 * 10^3\) = 11.3 / 470,000 = 2.404 * 10^-5 A) Tj
T*
(= 24.04 microA.) Tj
T*
ET

